<DocsLayout
  title="Build the plugin from source — Apple Docs"
  description="Developer and debug path: build Apple for Studio from the repo with Rojo and load it as a local plugin. Not the way to install Apple."
  heading="Build the plugin from source"
>
  {/*
    THIS PAGE BUILT THE WRONG PLUGIN, AND THE WRONG ONE IS THE BANNED ONE.

    It said "The plugin lives in apps/plugin" and gave `rojo build apps/plugin/…`. On 2026-09-19
    `apps/plugin` was declared legacy: its `handlers.run_code` compiles text that arrived over HTTP
    into a ModuleScript and `require`s it, which is the pattern the Creator Store asset requirements
    prohibit, and the asset built from that source was removed by Roblox for "Misusing Roblox
    Systems". The decision reached the CI workflows — plugin-release.yml carries a STALE TARGET
    banner, ci.yml renamed its artifact `apple-plugin-pr-unverified` — and never reached this page,
    which is the only build instruction the product publishes to a customer. So the one plugin a
    reader could obtain by following our own words was the one that executes code it was sent, while
    the landing page promised "It will not run code it was sent" and /docs/plugin promised the same.

    The CI paragraph went with it. That job still builds `apps/plugin`, so pointing a reader at its
    artifact hands them the legacy binary under the new name; and the artifact it named,
    `apple-plugin`, has not existed since ci.yml was renamed. Building locally is now the only
    instruction here, because it is the only one that yields the plugin this product ships.

    apps/site/tests/build-from-source-target.test.mjs fails if this page names apps/plugin again.
  */}
  <div class="callout callout--violet">
    <p>
      <strong>This is not how you install Apple.</strong> It is the development and debugging path,
      for people changing the plugin's code. If you want to <em>use</em> Apple, go to
      <a href="/docs/plugin">Install Apple for Studio</a> — the Creator Store is the only supported
      install path, and a hand-built plugin is unsupported, unsigned and updated by nobody but you.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Build <code>apps/apple-plugin</code> and nothing else.</strong> There is a second
      Studio plugin in the repository, <code>apps/plugin</code>. It is the legacy source the removed
      Creator Store asset was built from, it executes Luau it receives over the wire, and it must not
      be loaded into a Studio you care about. Its own README says so in its first line.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Build it</h2>
  <p>
    The plugin this product ships lives in <code>apps/apple-plugin</code>. Build it with the
    repository's own script, which needs Node, <code>python3</code>,
    <a href="https://luau.org/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">luau-analyze</a> and
    <a href="https://rojo.space" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Rojo</a> — version
    <strong>7.7.0</strong>, the version this repository pins.
  </p>
  <pre><code>node apps/apple-plugin/scripts/build.mjs</code></pre>
  <p>
    It parses every source, refuses a <code>require</code> of a module that is not bundled, runs
    Rojo, and then reads the <em>built</em> artifact back — scanning it for credentials and
    developer paths, and checking that the capabilities the source claims are actually in the bytes.
    That last step exists because the legacy build once shipped with a version string and a feature
    set its own source disagreed with, and every test passed because every test read the
    <code>.luau</code>. The result is
    <code>apps/apple-plugin/release/apple-studio.rbxm</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Load it as a local plugin</h2>
  <p>Drop the <code>.rbxm</code> into Studio's local plugins folder and restart Studio:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Windows:</strong> <code>%LOCALAPPDATA%\Roblox\Plugins</code></li>
    <li><strong>macOS:</strong> <code>~/Documents/Roblox/Plugins</code></li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Studio's <strong>Plugins</strong> tab has a <strong>Plugins Folder</strong> button that opens
    the same directory, which is the reliable way to find it if the path above does not exist yet.
    Remove a local build by deleting the file and restarting Studio.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      <strong>Do not run both at once.</strong> A local build and a Creator Store install are two
      separate plugins as far as Studio is concerned. Running both gives you two Apple buttons, two
      panels and two pollers fighting over the same place. Disable or remove one.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Iterating on the code</h2>
  <p>
    For live editing, Roblox's own workflow beats rebuilding: enable
    <strong>Plugin Debugging Enabled</strong> in Studio's settings, use
    <strong>Save as Local Plugin</strong> from the <strong>Plugins</strong> menu, and work from the
    copy inside <code>PluginDebugService</code>. Right-click it for
    <strong>Save and Reload Plugin</strong> to apply changes without restarting Studio.
  </p>
  <p>
    One behavioural difference worth knowing while debugging: our plugin source notes that a
    locally-installed plugin does not go through Studio's per-plugin HTTP permission prompt, while
    a Creator Store install does. That is an observation about our own builds, not a documented
    Roblox guarantee — so test the permission path against a real store install before trusting it,
    and never assume users get the frictionless version you do.
  </p>

  <h2>Publishing an update</h2>
  <p>
    Releases are cut by hand, from Studio: <strong>Plugins</strong> menu →
    <strong>Publish as Plugin</strong> → <strong>Overwrite an existing asset</strong>, selecting the
    existing Apple asset so the id stays stable. There is no automated path: Roblox's Open Cloud
    asset API does not support the Plugin asset type at all, and its update endpoint can only
    replace content for models. Budget a human in Studio for every version.
  </p>
  <p>
    Users do not receive that update automatically — see <a href="/docs/updating">Updating</a>.
    Assume a long tail of installs running old builds, permanently, and keep the server tolerant of
    them.
  </p>

</DocsLayout>
